(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-pathophysiology-i-exam-2-cp-exam-2-master-exam-form-a"]={"title":"Clin Path I Exam 2 Master Exam — Form A","category":"Clinical Pathophysiology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Where does deep vein thrombosis rank among cardiovascular causes of death?","choices":["Third most common","First most common","Tenth most common","It is not a significant cause of death"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Deep vein thrombosis, a clot in a deep vein typically in the legs, is the third most common cause of death from cardiovascular diseases, so the venous side is not the minor partner it might appear. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"How does nephrotic syndrome cause a pleural effusion?","choices":["It raises vascular hydrostatic pressure","It lowers pleural space pressure","It inflames the pleural membrane","It lowers plasma oncotic pressure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Decreased plasma oncotic pressure, from severe hypoalbuminemia, nephrotic syndrome (protein loss) or cirrhosis (less albumin synthesis), lets fluid collect in the pleural space. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slides 93, 94"},{"q":"What does von Willebrand factor do?","choices":["Activates factor X together with factor IXa","Degrades fibrin once a clot has formed","Supports platelet adhesion and stabilizes factor VIII","Limits coagulation together with protein S"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Von Willebrand factor supports platelet adhesion and carries and stabilizes factor VIII, which is why von Willebrand disease is listed with the hemophilias as a bleeding disorder. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"What are the two main pathogenic mechanisms of vasculitis?","choices":["Immune-mediated inflammation, and pathogen invasion","Lipid accumulation and calcification","Mechanical stress and loss of elastic tissue","Valve insufficiency and venous stasis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the distinction matters because the wall is being destroyed in one case by the host's own response and in the other by the organism itself. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What is a vegetation?","choices":["A calcified nodule on the valve annulus","Microorganisms enmeshed in fibrin deposits","A ballooned segment of valve leaflet","A thrombus within the left atrial appendage"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. As they enlarge they interfere with valve function and predispose to embolism, so the lesion does harm in two distinct ways. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Why is squamous cell carcinoma of the lung often detected earlier than other lung cancers?","choices":["Sputum cytology can show it","It doubles in about 33 days","It arises in the lung periphery","Its large cells form visible nests"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Two-thirds arise in the central bronchi near the hilus as intraluminal growth, so sputum cytology shows the cancer and detection comes earlier. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slides 25, 26, 27"},{"q":"What is the cause of factor V Leiden thrombophilia?","choices":["An autoimmune response","A factor V deficiency","An immune reaction to heparin","A genetic mutation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Factor V Leiden is a thrombophilia (excessive clotting) caused by a genetic mutation; antiphospholipid syndrome, the other thrombophilia listed, is an autoimmune response. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which pair converts prothrombin to thrombin?","choices":["Factor IXa with factor VIIIa","Tissue factor with factor VIIa","Factor Xa with factor Va","Factor XIIIa with fibrin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In thrombin generation, Xa with its cofactor Va converts prothrombin (II) to thrombin (IIa); thrombin then turns fibrinogen (I) into fibrin. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slides 43, 44"},{"q":"How does interstitial fluid enter a lymphatic capillary?","choices":["Through smooth muscle pores in the wall","By active pumping across a basement membrane","Between overlapping endothelial cells","Driven in by the heart's systolic pressure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The capillary wall is built of overlapping endothelial cells whose edges act as valves: they open to let interstitial fluid in and close behind it, so flow runs one way. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which components assemble the membrane attack complex?","choices":["C1 with C4 and C2","C3a and C5a","Factor B and factor D","C5b with C6–C9"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. C5b initiates assembly of the terminal components C6–C9, and the assembled complex forms pores in a susceptible target membrane. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slides 68, 71"},{"q":"What does anemia reduce?","choices":["The blood's plasma volume","The blood's clotting factor supply","The marrow's white-cell output","The blood's oxygen-carrying capacity"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Anemia reduces the blood's oxygen-carrying capacity, which is why it is defined by hemoglobin below the cutoff for age, sex and pregnancy status. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which immunoglobulin crosses the placenta?","choices":["IgM","IgG","IgA","IgE"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. IgG crosses the placenta and provides maternal protection; it is also the most abundant serum class. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slides 44, 46"},{"q":"Which determinants of workload increase myocardial oxygen demand?","choices":["Coronary perfusion pressure and collateral circulation","Heart rate, preload, afterload and contractility","Plaque size and composition","Serum lipoprotein concentration"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Raising any one of the four raises demand, which is why ischemia can appear with the coronary anatomy entirely unchanged. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What does alpha-1 antitrypsin normally do in the lung?","choices":["Secretes surfactant into the alveoli","Releases proteases from neutrophils","Inhibits breakdown of alveolar tissue","Clears mucus from the bronchi"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Alpha-1 antitrypsin is a protective enzyme that inhibits proteolytic breakdown of alveolar tissue; its hereditary deficiency causes about 1% of emphysema. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slides 53, 54"},{"q":"What characterizes a saccular cerebral aneurysm?","choices":["Uniform dilation of the entire vessel circumference","A lesion arising from infected emboli","A blood-filled channel within the wall layers","A thin-walled protrusion lacking elastic lamina"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, with very thin or absent tunica media. The lack of elastic lamina is named as the MAIN feature, and these are typically acquired rather than congenital. The tunica media is very thin or absent. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Approximately what share of serum immunoglobulin is IgG?","choices":["Less than 1%","5–10%","10–15%","75–80%"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. IgG is the most abundant class, approximately 75–80% of serum immunoglobulin (700–1700 mg/dL); reference intervals vary by laboratory. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Where do T-cell precursors develop?","choices":["Bone marrow","Germinal centers","Thymus","Spleen"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lymphoid progenitor branches into the B-cell precursor in the bone marrow and the T-cell precursor in the thymus, the origin of T-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia/lymphoma. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which condition can blunt the reticulocyte response to hemolysis?","choices":["Renal disease","Rising erythropoietin","Recovery from anemia","Dehydration"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. With hemolysis, erythropoietin and reticulocyte production usually rise when the marrow can compensate, but the response may be inadequate with marrow disease, nutrient deficiency, renal disease or infection. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Myasthenia gravis causes acute respiratory failure through which category of cause?","choices":["Central nervous system","Chest skeletal system","Neuromuscular disease","Pulmonary parenchyma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Neuromuscular causes are multiple sclerosis, myasthenia gravis, polio, Guillain-Barré syndrome and quadriplegia. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What are the final two steps of the atherosclerotic sequence?","choices":["Monocyte adhesion, then platelet adhesion","Lipid accumulation, then calcification","Endothelial injury, then lipoprotein accumulation","T cell recruitment, then smooth muscle migration"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. After smooth muscle proliferation, matrix production and T cell recruitment, the sequence closes with lipid accumulation and then calcification of the extracellular matrix and necrotic debris. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which disorder is one of the myeloproliferative neoplasms?","choices":["Immune thrombocytopenia","Essential thrombocythemia","Chronic lymphocytic leukemia","Hereditary spherocytosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The myeloproliferative neoplasms are chronic myeloid leukemia, polycythemia vera, essential thrombocythemia and primary myelofibrosis: clonal proliferations of myeloid cells. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What is opsonization?","choices":["Coating that eases ingestion by phagocytes","Clumping of particles into a lattice","Pore formation in a target membrane","Blocking a toxin from its target"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In opsonization antibody joins antigen on the cell's surface, and this coating facilitates ingestion of the antigen by a phagocyte. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which three causes should a raised eosinophil count bring to mind first?","choices":["Chronic infections such as tuberculosis","Acute bacterial inflammation and sepsis","Allergy, parasites and drug effects","Marrow malignancy before anything else"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Think of three things with increased eosinophils: allergy (especially asthma), a parasite, or a drug effect. Eosinophilia can rarely be neoplastic or autoimmune. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slides 12, 13"},{"q":"In leukemia, what can the peripheral white blood cell count be?","choices":["High, because blasts spill into the blood","Low, because blasts stay in the marrow","Normal until leukostasis develops","Low, normal or high"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The peripheral white blood cell count in leukemia may be low, normal or high, because marrow involvement can suppress normal blood-cell production while malignant cells may or may not spill into the blood. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slides 21, 22"},{"q":"Which defenses belong to innate immunity?","choices":["Complement and interferons","Memory B cells from a past exposure","Antibodies left by a past vaccine","Cytotoxic T cells with specific receptors"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Innate immunity acts without prior sensitization and includes skin and mucosal barriers, phagocytes, complement and interferons, with natural killer cells as its cytotoxic defense. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What is the initiating feature of myocardial infarction?","choices":["Gradual narrowing of the lumen over years","Vasospasm of an angiographically normal artery","Inflammation of the pericardium","Thrombosis on an ulcerated or cracked atherosclerotic plaque"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The plaque has to fail before the thrombus forms, which is why plaque stability matters more than plaque size. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What produces the color sequence in Raynaud phenomenon?","choices":["Hemorrhage into the skin followed by resorption","Deposition of immune complexes in dermal vessels","Progressive thrombosis of digital arteries","Vasoconstriction, anoxia, then reperfusion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the sequence maps onto the colors in order: white to blue to red. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which deficiency causes megaloblastic anemia?","choices":["Iron deficiency","Liver disease or alcohol","Thalassemia","Vitamin B12 or folate deficiency"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In the macrocytic group (mean cell volume above 100 fL, femtoliters), the megaloblastic causes are vitamin B12 or folate deficiency; liver disease, alcohol, drugs and reticulocytosis are the nonmegaloblastic causes. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"How do fever, infection and burns contribute to hypercapnic ventilatory failure?","choices":["They increase dead space","They raise carbon dioxide production","They block sensing of carbon dioxide","They cut the respiratory rate"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hypercapnic failure is alveolar ventilation inadequate for the carbon dioxide produced, and fever, infection and trauma or burns increase that production. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What is a pneumoconiosis?","choices":["Lung disease from inhaled organic agents","Granulomas from an unknown trigger","Infection from aspirated flora","Lung disease from inhaled inorganic dust"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pneumoconiosis is parenchymal lung disease caused by inhalation of inorganic dust particles such as coal, silica and asbestos. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"What is the main role of CD4 helper T cells?","choices":["Killing infected cells with perforin","Coordinating humoral and cellular responses","Secreting antibodies into plasma","Limiting activation to keep self-tolerance"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. CD4 helper T cells coordinate cell-mediated and humoral responses, using cytokines and cell contact to regulate other immune populations. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slides 24, 30"},{"q":"What do the macrophages within a plaque release?","choices":["Collagen and fibrin, which stabilize the plaque","Inflammatory mediators and growth factor","Enzymes that clear the lipid core entirely","Vasodilators that increase flow through the vessel"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Both types of macrophage, including the lipid-filled foam cells, release inflammatory mediators and growth factor. These attract more leukocytes and stimulate smooth muscle proliferation, so the lesion keeps recruiting cells and wall growth. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"How do the capillaries within the atherosclerotic arterial wall contribute to disruption?","choices":["They deliver macrophages that dissolve the fibrous cap","They drain lipid out of the core, collapsing the plaque","Their thin walls bleed","They constrict, producing local ischemia within the wall"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The plaque can be disrupted from the inside as well as from the lumen, which is a route easy to overlook. Depositing blood products and raising pressure within the plaque. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What characterizes the end-stage (destruction) pattern of diffuse interstitial lung disease?","choices":["Lost alveolar walls and stiff, noncompliant lungs","Hyperinflated lungs with air trapped in the sacs","Dilated bronchi that act as pockets of infection","Reversible inflammation filling the alveoli"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The destruction stage is end-stage disease: alveolar walls are lost and the scarred lungs become stiff and noncompliant. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"What feature defines the obstructive pulmonary diseases?","choices":["Increased airflow resistance","Decreased expansion of the lungs","Thickening of the alveolar interstitium","Air in the pleural space"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Obstructive pulmonary diseases are manifested by increased airflow resistance, whether from the airway wall, loss of parenchyma or material in the lumen. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What is an aneurysm?","choices":["Localized abnormal dilation of a blood vessel","Blood tunneling through the medial planes of the wall","An intimal lesion projecting into the lumen","An irregular direct connection between artery and vein"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It may be congenital or acquired, and it is classified by shape. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which condition is linked to abnormal transforming growth factor signaling and defective collagen synthesis?","choices":["Kawasaki disease","Marfan syndrome","Systemic lupus erythematosus","Tertiary syphilis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, listed under poor connective tissue quality producing weak vessel walls &mdash; a genetic route to the same wall failure that hypertension reaches mechanically. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Why is unstable angina included among the acute coronary syndromes?","choices":["It always involves complete vessel occlusion","It is caused by vasospasm rather than plaque","It occurs only in patients with prior infarction","It may progress to acute ischemia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It is grouped by where it is heading rather than by where it currently is, which is the point of treating it as acute. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which leukocyte is the most numerous first responder, acting by phagocytosis?","choices":["Eosinophil","Basophil","Neutrophil","Monocyte"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Neutrophils, the “poly” or PMN (polymorphonuclear leukocyte), are the most numerous first responders and act by phagocytosis. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What follows injury to the vessel at the start of the atherosclerotic sequence?","choices":["Accumulation of low-density lipoprotein in the vessel wall","Calcification of the extracellular matrix","Smooth muscle proliferation with matrix production","Formation of a fibrous cap over a lipid core"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and monocyte adhesion to the endothelium and platelet adhesion follow it before any smooth muscle is recruited. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which are named as superficial veins?","choices":["The femoral and popliteal veins","The portal and hepatic veins","The great and small saphenous veins","The jugular and subclavian veins"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the deep veins named are the femoral, popliteal and tibial. The anatomic location determines which pathology follows. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What does hypochromia of red cells reflect?","choices":["Reduced hemoglobinization","Reduced cell size","Abnormal cell shape","Increased cell volume"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The degree of hemoglobinization is reflected in the color of red cells: normochromic or hypochromic. Hypochromic cells carry less hemoglobin, as in the microcytic hypochromic anemias of impaired hemoglobin synthesis. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slides 31, 32"},{"q":"Raised blood pressure is a risk factor for which of these?","choices":["Atherosclerosis, heart failure and renal failure","Varicose veins and hemorrhoids","Deep vein thrombosis","Arteriovenous fistula"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Raised blood pressure is a risk factor for atherosclerosis, congestive heart failure and renal failure, and also for cerebral hemorrhage and aortic dissection. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"The Arthus reaction and serum sickness are examples of which hypersensitivity type?","choices":["Type I","Type II","Type III","Type IV"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Both are type III immune-complex reactions: the Arthus reaction is a local response in a previously sensitized person, and serum sickness is a systemic reaction to circulating immune complexes. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"What is restrictive cardiomyopathy most often related to?","choices":["A specific clinical condition","Alcohol and pregnancy","Coxsackievirus infection","Aging valvular calcification"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Restrictive cardiomyopathy is most often related to a specific clinical condition such as amyloidosis. Whatever the cause, the myocardium becomes fibrotic, rigid and noncompliant, restricting diastolic filling. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"Why does tension pneumothorax reduce cardiac output?","choices":["Air enters the coronary arteries","The shifted mediastinum cuts venous return","The lung re-expands too quickly","Fluid tamponades the heart"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Air enters but cannot escape on expiration and builds up under pressure; the ipsilateral lung collapses and the mediastinum is forced to the contralateral side, decreasing venous return and cardiac output. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slides 90, 91"},{"q":"What do vascular smooth muscle cells synthesize?","choices":["Immunoglobulins","Clotting factors for the plasma","Surfactant","Collagen, elastin, proteoglycans and cytokines"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, which is why they are central to both repair and disease: the same synthetic capacity that rebuilds a wall also builds an atherosclerotic plaque. Growth factors too. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"By what mechanism does rheumatic fever damage the heart?","choices":["Direct invasion of the valve leaflets by the organism","An immune attack arising from cross-reactivity after streptococcal infection","Calcium deposition on the leaflets","Ischemic necrosis of the papillary muscles"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The organism is long gone; what damages the heart is the immune response aimed at something that resembles it. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Through which chain does chronic bronchitis lead to cor pulmonale?","choices":["Left ventricular failure, pulmonary edema, right ventricular atrophy","Embolism, dead space, hypercapnia, left atrial enlargement","Hypoxic vasoconstriction, pulmonary hypertension, right ventricular dilation","Plaque rupture, coronary thrombosis, right ventricular infarction"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pulmonary artery vasoconstriction from hypoxia results in pulmonary hypertension, then right ventricular dilation (cor pulmonale) and right-sided heart failure. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"After a FIRST exposure to an antigen, which antibody class rises first?","choices":["IgG","IgM","IgA","IgE"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. On the primary response time course IgM rises and peaks first, and IgG starts later; on second exposure IgG dominates. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slides 37, 46"},{"q":"Which surface marker is found on T cells as a group?","choices":["CD3","CD4","CD8","CD19"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. T cells carry CD3, with CD4 or CD8 on the major subsets. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"At what degree of closure does stenosis begin to affect hemodynamics?","choices":["About ten percent","About seventy-five percent","About fifty percent","At complete closure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hemodynamics are affected once the valve is about fifty percent closed. The narrowing creates a pressure gradient across the valve and extra pressure work, because blood must be forced through a smaller opening. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"How does pericardial disease usually arise?","choices":["As a primary condition in most cases","Mainly as a consequence of myocardial infarction","Secondary to another primary cause","Mainly from direct infection of the pericardium"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pericardial disease is rarely a primary condition and is usually secondary to another primary cause. Whatever the cause, fluid accumulates in the pericardial sac and painful inflammation of the pericardial structures occurs. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"Why must both the relative and the absolute white cell counts be read?","choices":["A fall in one type can falsely raise another's percentage","The relative count gives the number of cells per microliter","Each cell's percentage is measured apart from the others","The absolute count ignores the total white blood cell count"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Percentages are shares of one total, so an absolute decrease in one cell type can show an artificially increased relative count in another. Reading the absolute count shows whether that cell really rose. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What are the consequences of myocardial ischemia?","choices":["Abnormal rhythm only, with function preserved throughout","Abnormal heart function and abnormal rhythm","Immediate irreversible damage in every case","Valvular incompetence as the first consequence"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Two immediate consequences and one that depends on duration, which is why time is the variable that decides how much muscle is lost. With irreversible damage if prolonged. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What happens in mitral regurgitation?","choices":["Blood flows from atrium to ventricle during systole","Blood leaks from the aorta into the ventricle during diastole","Flow across the valve is obstructed during diastole","Blood flows back from ventricle to atrium during systole"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and because the leak occurs during systole, part of every beat is wasted going the wrong way. Raising left atrial volume and pressure. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"What does the adventitia contain?","choices":["A single layer of endothelium on a basement membrane","Lamellar units of elastin and smooth muscle","Nothing &mdash; it is a potential space","Loose connective tissue, sometimes with nerve fibers"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and in large vessels it carries its own small arterioles, which perfuse the adventitia and part of the media &mdash; the wall is too thick to be fed from the lumen alone. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which cough pattern defines chronic bronchitis?","choices":["Productive cough over 3 weeks after a viral illness","Productive cough over 3 months a year for 2 years","Dry nighttime cough for 2 months","Purulent sputum from infected bronchial pockets"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Chronic bronchitis is hypersecretion of bronchial mucus with a chronic or recurrent productive cough of more than 3 months each year for 2 or more successive years. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"How does pulmonary embolism push toward hypercapnic ventilatory failure?","choices":["It increases carbon dioxide production","It increases dead space","It blunts sensing of carbon dioxide","It blocks nerve signals to the muscles"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Increased dead space (wasted ventilation), from hypovolemia, low cardiac output or pulmonary embolism, leaves ventilation inadequate for the carbon dioxide produced. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slides 10, 14"},{"q":"Which T cells limit immune activation and maintain self-tolerance?","choices":["Cytotoxic T cells","Helper T cells","Regulatory T cells","Memory T cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Regulatory T cells limit immune activation and maintain self-tolerance; many are CD4 cells expressing FOXP3. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 32"}]};
